  "executive_briefing": "The global corporate tax and international trade landscape in mid-2026 is defined by a deep tension between multilateral tax harmonization and unilateral trade protectionism. Under the OECD Pillar Two G20 Inclusive Framework, over 140 countries are actively integrating the 15% global minimum tax on multinational enterprises, driving extensive corporate compliance programs. In contrast, unilateral trade policies face mounting legal friction, highlighted by a US Court for International Trade ruling that certain emergency tariffs are unlawful, even as effective tariffs are projected to settle at 15-18%. Regionally, ASEAN hubs are maintaining competitive corporate environments, with Singapore anchoring its 17% corporate income tax rate alongside partial exemptions. At the operational level, tax compliance is transforming through Continuous Transaction Controls (CTCs) and digitized billing standards, led by Singapore's phased GST InvoiceNow mandates for 2026.",
